    <item>
      <title>Dartmouth's provost warned us about AI cheating—but his own AI use cost him his job</title>
      <author>
                    Nate Anderson
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/dartmouths-provost-warned-us-about-ai-cheating-but-his-own-ai-use-cost-him-his-job/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/dartmouths-provost-warned-us-about-ai-cheating-but-his-own-ai-use-cost-him-his-job/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">23db8e0a529d705fdfaebc343b338935815236c4</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:40:13 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         People who love AI don&amp;#8217;t always love telling others they used it.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2228700612-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2228700612-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2228700612-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2228700612-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2228700612-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       Dartmouth's New Hampshire campus.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Getty Images                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;div class="page-anchor-wrapper"&gt;&lt;a class="" name="page-1" data-page="1" data-url="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/dartmouths-provost-warned-us-about-ai-cheating-but-his-own-ai-use-cost-him-his-job/" href="https://arstechnica.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;On August 9, 2026, Dartmouth College provost Santiago Schnell wrote a &lt;a href="https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2026/08/09/university-fight-against-ai-cheating-doesnt-go-far-enough/"&gt;Washington Post op-ed&lt;/a&gt; called “Universities are fighting AI cheating. But there’s a deeper problem.” This week, Schnell discovered the depths of the problem himself as he resigned from the university after an advisory committee &lt;a href="https://president.dartmouth.edu/sites/president/files/2026-10/Advisory%20Committee%20Report%20to%20President%202026-10-27.pdf"&gt;found&lt;/a&gt; that he failed to disclose significant AI use in an academic paper and in the Washington Post op-ed, even though publishers required such disclosure.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Schnell didn’t make it especially easy for the committee to investigate his AI use; the report uses the term “insufficient cooperation.” He did turn over his Dartmouth laptop, but he did not turn over logs from his personal LLM accounts related to the writings in question. Nor did he agree to meet with the committee.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The committee did get access to the Claude enterprise account Schnell had used through Dartmouth, though. This shows him using the LLM as a tool for revising his papers, finding and fixing bugs in code, and editing or generating figures. For instance, Schnell would run his papers through Claude and ask things like:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;I am getting read[y] to submit the enclosed paper, its supplementary materials and the enclosed repo to ChemSystemsChem. This paper is not biological, but rather chemical. Imagine that you are a reviewer for this paper with expertise in nonlinear chemical dynamics and mathematical chemical kinetics. What would be your referee report for this paper?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;This is quite a bit different from asking Claude to write your entire paper for you, and Schnell has long insisted that he used AI as an editorial aid and discussion partner. He told Semafor that he had used chatbots “to refine a few arguments” and “check for grammatical errors.” He told one journal editor that he “used AI as an editorial tool” to “help refine sentences, improve clarity, and the expression of arguments.” He had not disclosed his AI use to an academic journal, he said, because he “did not understand that this type of AI-assisted editing required disclosure.”&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;Indeed, Schnell’s own description of what he did with AI doesn’t sound, on the long list of AI-powered sins, like it would rank near the top. But it’s hard to know. As the Dartmouth committee points out, the full scope of Schnell’s AI use was unclear to them. The committee believes that Schnell’s public statements may understate “the nature of the Provost’s actual AI use” and says that Schnell regularly “went beyond his other public descriptions of his use of AI in writing.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;We do know that Schnell also asked LLMs to take &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TeX"&gt;TeX files&lt;/a&gt; and “make the corrections to the files, and the repo. If you have to generate new figures, please provide them to me. Let me know what you change in the repo, and text files so that I can take a look into it.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Whatever you think of his actions, Schnell repeatedly failed to disclose them. It is certainly possible that he thought his AI use was on par with a spelling or grammar checker, though one Claude exchange raises questions about whether Schnell wanted to hide traces of LLM activity in his work. From the committee report:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Provost Schnell’s Claude logs reflect that he uploaded to Claude a manuscript of a forthcoming article entitled &lt;em&gt;Academic Freedom and Catholic Mission: The Procedural Overlap Zone in University Governance&lt;/em&gt; and prompted Claude: “Are there many signatures of AI writing in this article?” Schnell later submitted a revised manuscript that incorporated the kinds of changes Claude suggested.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Schnell has now resigned. Dartmouth, though, is barreling ahead with AI. The university calls itself the place that “launched the field of artificial intelligence” back in 1956, when “the term was coined and the foundational question was asked: Can machines solve problems once reserved for human minds?” Seventy years later, Dartmouth is &lt;a href="https://ai.dartmouth.edu/ai-70-events/what-must-remain-human-conference/ai-70-student-hackathon"&gt;hosting a conference&lt;/a&gt; called “What Must Remain Human?” that will feature a student hackathon sponsored by Anthropic and speakers like Mira Murati and Yann LeCun.&lt;/p&gt;                                                      &lt;p&gt;Despite the scandal, Dartmouth President Sian Leah Beilock wants more AI. (She wrote a &lt;a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/2026/09/universities-prohibiting-ai-classroom/688509/"&gt;piece for The Atlantic&lt;/a&gt; in September called “Why I Want More AI at Dartmouth.”) What’s crucial, though, is that the school’s leaders should “hold themselves to the highest standards,” she said. This appears to mean one main thing: disclosure.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://president.dartmouth.edu/news/2026/10/update-provost-review"&gt;announcing Schnell’s resignation&lt;/a&gt;, Beilock wrote that “each of us brings a different perspective on how and when to incorporate AI into our work and our lives. Moreover, keeping Dartmouth at the forefront of AI inevitably means leading through an environment that is fluid and fast-changing. That is why, as leaders, it is so critical that we model the transparency and human judgment that we expect from our students and scholars.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="page-anchor-wrapper"&gt;&lt;a class="record-pageview" name="page-2" data-page="2" data-url="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/dartmouths-provost-warned-us-about-ai-cheating-but-his-own-ai-use-cost-him-his-job/" href="https://arstechnica.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Who wrote this?&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;What does “disclosure” mean? In a &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.13660"&gt;September 2026 paper&lt;/a&gt; on AI detection tools, called “Who Wrote This?”, Schnell himself goes to great lengths to argue that “human-written” or “AI-written” is far too simple a binary. In his view, everything will become a mix of human judgment and AI work, and there’s nothing wrong with that. The key for him is that the human author takes full responsibility for the text, however it was composed and edited.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In the paper, Schnell lays out his method for producing it. It’s worth quoting at some length:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;In September 2026, I asked OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Pro whether an AI-written essay could ever be identified with certainty, and I suggested connecting the problem to Turing’s imitation game and to the “reverse” Turing test posed by AI-text detection. GPT-5.6 Pro connected the question to binary hypothesis testing and statistical decision theory, proposed early structures and titles, identified candidate literature, and generated portions of early drafts. I chose the problem, journal, audience, and eventual direction of the argument. I decided which examples and results should remain central, including the decision to retain the Liang study while confronting it with important counterevidence. I checked the cited literature, rejected or corrected claims that I judged inaccurate, corrected mathematical and conceptual errors, and reorganized the argument.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In revising the manuscript, I rewrote most sections and added substantial explanations. I occasionally asked GPT-5.6 Sol to provide language-editing assistance for sections I had rewritten. I created the figures and made the final decisions about the mathematics, interpretation, sources, organization, and wording. I take full responsibility for the resulting manuscript.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Before submitting the manuscript for publication, I also asked GPT-5.6 Pro and Anthropic Claude Fable 5.1 to review it critically and identify issues that reviewers might raise. I evaluated the issues they identified and made corrections where I judged them warranted.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Despite all this assistance, Schnell resists the idea that AI is a “co-author.” Instead, he writes, “Calling [the paper] ‘coauthored’ would introduce a different confusion. Mathematical tools have long made substantive contributions without thereby becoming authors… The point is not that the software contributed nothing. It is that contribution and authorship are different roles.”&lt;/p&gt;                                                      &lt;p&gt;He is not against disclosure, as he makes clear by describing his method. Yet curiously, when submitting other papers for publication, he did not disclose his AI use even when required by publishers.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Confused about the rules in an age where even universities are pushing AI? So are many students. &lt;a href="https://home.dartmouth.edu/news/2026/10/dartmouth-survey-reveals-what-students-think-about-ai"&gt;New survey data&lt;/a&gt; unveiled at this weekend’s conference shows that 61 percent of college students think AI helps explain difficult concepts, and half believe that they produce better work after talking through issues with AI tools. But “70 percent of the college students in the survey indicate that take-home work has become unfair due to AI, with 39 percent feeling pressured to use it because their peers do.” Among Dartmouth students, 92 percent of them fear “that AI can hurt writing skills.” And many of them believe their peers are using far more AI than they actually are.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Not everyone is racked with existential doubt over how to deal with AI, though. Dartmouth student Eli Moyse &lt;a href="https://www.thedartmouth.com/article/2026/09/moyse-fire-provost-santiago-schnell"&gt;wrote in a September op-ed&lt;/a&gt; that Schnell should be fired because “a fetishization of AI to earn publicity for the College has rotted the brain of our top academic.”&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>ai</category>
      <category>dartmouth</category>
      <category>high-ed</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Over 25% of people lie to get antibiotics—and their lies aren't even good</title>
      <author>
                    Beth Mole
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/10/over-25-of-people-lie-to-get-antibiotics-and-their-lies-arent-even-good/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/10/over-25-of-people-lie-to-get-antibiotics-and-their-lies-arent-even-good/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">1b0ed7e6447f0b64ab400ec584a9ee11b6d882f1</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:17:50 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Faked symptoms still point to infections that antibiotics don&amp;#8217;t treat.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1219036990-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="A woman blows her nose into a tissue while a doctor stands before her." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1219036990-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1219036990-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1219036990-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1219036990-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/woman-visiting-doctor-royalty-free-image/1219036990?phrase=sick%20patient%20talking%20to%20doctor&amp;amp;searchscope=image,film&amp;amp;adppopup=true"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Getty | AnnaStills                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;div class="page-anchor-wrapper"&gt;&lt;a class="" name="page-1" data-page="1" data-url="https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/10/over-25-of-people-lie-to-get-antibiotics-and-their-lies-arent-even-good/" href="https://arstechnica.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;A significant number of patients in the US lie to their doctors to get antibiotics they likely don’t need, according to two new studies. While that may not be a startling revelation, the studies drills down on the phenomenon, finding that the lies patients tell are generally bad ones—and yet, they work almost all the time.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In the &lt;a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2856032"&gt;two&lt;/a&gt; linked &lt;a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2856033"&gt;studies&lt;/a&gt; published Friday in JAMA Network Open, a team of researchers led by Alistair Thorpe at the University of Utah analyzed online survey data from over 1,000 adults. They found that over a quarter of people reported having lied to a clinician at least once to get an antibiotic prescription for themselves or a child in their care. Patients lied by either exaggerating symptoms, making up symptoms, or—most often—both.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But interestingly, the lies people told to get antibiotics involved making up or exaggerating symptoms of infections that wouldn’t be treated with antibiotics. For example, the most common falsehoods were symptoms associated with colds, such as fever, cough, and sore throat. That’s despite the fact that colds are caused by viruses, not bacteria—which antibiotics treat.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Further, while the data suggests many patients were being dishonest, clinicians responded with what could be considered their own form of dishonesty; in most cases, the dubious fibs led to antibiotic prescriptions. Specifically, 77 percent of adult patients and 87 percent of caregivers said their lies worked, leading them to get the antibiotic prescriptions they wanted.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“These findings signal a significant issue for health professionals making antibiotic prescribing decisions and for the fields of antimicrobial stewardship and public health,” Thorpe and colleagues concluded.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Detailed deception&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;For the study, the researchers reviewed survey responses from 1,371 online respondents and found 959 responses that were valid and complete. Of those, 268 (28 percent) respondents reported misrepresenting their symptoms to get antibiotics. Of those, 108 reported exaggerating symptoms, 19 reported fabricating symptoms, and 141 reported doing both. In a separate analysis of 263 respondents who identified as caregivers, 63 (27 percent) reported misrepresenting symptoms of a child to get antibiotics.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;The most common reported reasons for lying were that people: felt their illness or the illness of a child in their care was particularly severe; believed that antibiotics were the only thing that would help them; believed antibiotics would help them return to responsibilities (work/school) faster; or felt they should have personal autonomy (thinking they know more about their health status than the clinician or feeling like they should get to decide their own treatment).&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Those who misrepresented their symptoms tended to be younger and feel their symptoms were not taken seriously by clinicians. They were more likely to believe that antibiotic resistance is a hoax, such as a scare tactic by the government or a gimmick by drug companies to justify charging more for antibiotics. People who lied were also more likely to have more underlying conditions, view lying as more acceptable, and report needing help reading health-related materials. On the other hand, those who didn’t report misrepresenting their symptoms tended to have greater antibiotic knowledge and aversion to taking antibiotics.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;For people who reported misrepresenting symptoms, the online survey gave them additional questions about the types of symptoms they fabricated or exaggerated. The most common falsehoods, reported by 65 percent of dishonest patients, were to misrepresent cold-related symptoms. After that were misrepresented stomach symptoms (49 percent), joint or muscle pain (46 percent), eye symptoms (34 percent), rashes and other skin conditions (32.5 percent), urinary symptoms (31 percent), or a general feeling of being unwell (37 percent). Respondents could also report not remembering what they lied about.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="page-anchor-wrapper"&gt;&lt;a class="record-pageview" name="page-2" data-page="2" data-url="https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/10/over-25-of-people-lie-to-get-antibiotics-and-their-lies-arent-even-good/" href="https://arstechnica.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;“To be clear, none of these are specific indications for antibiotics,” Jeffrey Linder, of Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine, wrote in &lt;a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2856037"&gt;an accompanying commentary&lt;/a&gt;, highlighting that these lies shouldn’t have helped patients get unnecessary antibiotics. “In fact, of the 8 categories offered that patients could have exaggerated or made up to get antibiotics, only 1 of them (urinary symptoms) clearly justified antibiotic prescribing,” he wrote.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;While symptoms of respiratory infections can be due to bacterial infections in some situations—namely, acute sinus infection, strep throat, or pneumonia—those conditions have specific clinical guidance and/or associated tests to confirm the diagnosis, Linder highlights.&lt;/p&gt;                                                      &lt;h2&gt;Dishonesty all around&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Given the magnitude of the deception, the findings raise concern that the ill-gotten antibiotics could contribute to the rise of antibiotic-resistant infections. Misuse and overuse of antibiotics are known to &lt;a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance"&gt;drive the development and spread of drug resistance&lt;/a&gt;. This week, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention released &lt;a href="https://arpsp.cdc.gov/artr/antimicrobial-resistance-threats#urgent-threats"&gt;an updated report on antimicrobial resistance threats&lt;/a&gt;, finding that drug-resistant bacteria and fungi are now causing 9.7 million infections and 50,800 deaths in the US each year.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But in the accompanying commentary, Linder said that an even more concerning consequence may be the “erosion of trust” between clinicians and patients. Linder acknowledged that drug resistance is a concern, but said that on the basis of an individual patient, in most clinical circumstances, the development of drug resistance is “probably irrelevant.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;To Linder, the more pertinent question doctors must face is: “Is this prescription more likely to help my patient or hurt them?” The answer is that it’s more likely to hurt; unnecessary antibiotics provide no benefit, but pose risk of adverse events, allergic reactions, and disruption of healthy microbiomes, which can provide an opening for opportunistic pathogens, such as &lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://arpsp.cdc.gov/artr/cdiff"&gt;Clostridioides difficile&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/em&gt;(which is classified as a “serious threat” in the CDC’s newly updated antimicrobial resistance threat report.)&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“For these non-antibiotic-appropriate diagnoses, prescribing antibiotics is medically incorrect and could itself be considered a form of dishonesty or lying mainly to assuage the patient,” Linder wrote. That dishonesty is corrosive, he said, quoting philosopher Sissela Bok, who wrote extensively about lying and, even specifically, about lying to get antibiotics. In 1978, Bok noted that antibiotics are “sometimes used as placebos” and many parents “come to believe that they must ask for the prescription of antibiotics every time their child has a fever or a cold.” But Bok argued that when clinicians prescribe medications more likely to harm than help, such “practices lacking in candor” threaten “the entire institution of medicine.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Linder concludes by reminding readers that “Clinicians’ obligation is to act in patients’ best interest, even if patients are hoping we do something different, even if patients do not like it, or even if patients lie about their symptoms.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Thorpe and colleagues, meanwhile, conclude with a call to address the reasons patients lie in the first place, writing: “Strategies to improve communication about antibiotics are needed to address patient concerns, support understanding of appropriate antibiotic use, and strengthen patient-clinician relationships.”&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>antibiotics</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Basically no one is using AI agents for their DoorDash orders</title>
      <author>
                    Kyle Orland
